l send each other notes that's  how we argue notes or emails saying this  really should be in for the next  copyright update or we're late on adding  this we should add it but rarely rarely  disagree with each other are there  political considerations when deciding  about whether a word should or shouldn't  be in the dictionary when I say  political that could apply to you know  sort of like partisan politics as we  know it but also issues of political  correctness for example I mean are these  issues that you contend with in deciding  about whether a word should be in the  dictionary not so much in deciding on  whether word should be in the dictionary  that's a pretty basic data approach but  that does come into play when we're  evaluating the raw material that we use  in writing definitions lexicographers  get very very good at reading through  political spin on all sides they get  very good at sort of gauging when  someone is using a word and you hear a  lot from one side or the other saying  well that's a ridiculous word you  shouldn't enter it or that's politically  correct you get very good at sort of  filtering out the opinion and just  coming right down to the word itself and  the great thing about online  dictionaries is we can even write  paragraphs saying like people object to  this use because they see it as overly  politicized which we couldn't do in  print dictionaries well along those  lines I mean it seems like the  definitions of words are what maybe lend  themselves more to disagreement and and  bias or being politicized right the you  could probably have more agreement about  the inclusion or exclusion of a word but  then choosing how that word is defined  that seems to me to be a little dicey  err it is and I think it's also an  ever-evolving process just like language  outside of the dictionary office move so  does the language that you use in  writing a dictionary definition I am  working on updating some definitions  from a dictionary that was published  about sixty years ago and a lot of the  work that I'm doing and half  doing or things like D gendering words  and definitions that don't need to be  gendered making sure that there's parity  in terms of if we enter chairman we  should also enter chairwoman so those  sorts of things back in the 50s when  this dictionary was being edited that  was you know it would be  forward-thinking to include the word  chairwoman now it's just a given so in  terms of not necessarily the language  that that gets in but yeah the language  that gets used in definitions that  involves just as much as the language  that gets used in the world when we are  defining words and this would also apply  to phrases and I'll give an example when  can we say that the meaning has changed  sort of due to common usage and a good  example that I can think of is the  phrase to beg the question right begging  the question originally is a sort of  logical fallacy where you're using the  premise to support a conclusion but now  it's used colloquially and I guess I  would I would say incorrectly in some  sense by people to say well that raises  the question or it makes a particular  question obvious or puts it out in the  forefront it's not really what begging  the question originally meant but it's  it's been misused so often that one  could argue and I'm starting to seeing  it and see it in some online  dictionaries that it's sort of like a  secondary or tertiary meaning of that  phrase how do you figure something like  that out like when does it become  correct right use it that way well the  idea of what's correct  and what's incorrect certainly varies  from person to person  um but within lexicography if a new  meaning meets those criterion or the  criteria then it's valid for it's valid  for entry so beg the question this is a  great example there are lots of people  who say you know this raise the question  sense is wrong because it's not the  original meaning and that's true but  honestly we have you know decades and  decades of edited published prose where  beg the question is used to mean raised  the question so at that point it's not  so much an issue  of this is correct and this is incorrect  and we keep the incorrect stuff out the  dictionaries approach is if it's used  enough in print someone is probably  going to try and look it up at some  point and so we should enter it whether  people think it's correct or incorrect  and you can you can say you know this  sense of beg the question is considered  incorrect by some people and and that's  okay but I think people see the  dictionary as this sort of black and  white right and wrong Authority and so  when the meanings of words change and  the dictionary acknowledges that they  get very upset because they feel like  the dictionary is abrogating its  authority but we're doing what we've  always done which is just to record the  language as it's used let me ask you  about a controversy I was embroiled in a  few months ago I sort of offhandedly use  the word irregardless which I mean I  think basically means the same thing as  regardless and I looked it up and and  irregardless is a word but I got a lot  of emails from people saying David you  know you really hurt your credibility  when you said irregardless when talking  about you know something related to the  presidents or something like that right  where are we right now on irregardless  irregardless well I should start by  saying that in my book I call myself  America's foremost irregardless  apologist so this will this should set  it up for you  I mean irregardless is the word that's  been around in print for over 200 years  and it has used in some dialects of  English usually rural dialects of  English as a way as an intensive form of  regardless so you can say regardless of  this and irregardless we're doing blah  blah blah now the fact is is that even  though it's been in print for 200 years  it's still not a part of standard  English w